{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>You shall not bow to them.</b> As do the makers of forms, who think they can draw down the power of the supernal beings below for the need of the one." "<b>And you shall not serve them.</b> To slaughter and offer incense to them is included in 'You shall not have.' Likewise the warning of 'And the name of other elohim you shall not mention; it shall not be heard from your mouth' (below, 23:13), and in the book of Joshua, 'And you shall not make them swear' (Joshua 23:7). And the meaning of <b>El Qanna.</b> For it is justice, after HaShem created you and sustains you, how can you give His honor to another who neither benefits nor harms:" "And the meaning of El is to inform you that He is mighty and able to take vengeance from you at every moment, and you cannot escape Him. Jeremiah mentioned it, for 'he who eats meat, his teeth will be set on edge' (Jeremiah 31:29), and in the book of Ezekiel it is explicit, for the honored HaShem swore that 'a son shall not bear the iniquity of the father' (Ezekiel 18:20). If so, what is the meaning of 'visiting the iniquity of fathers upon sons'? And the answer is that Ezekiel explained: if the father was wicked and the son did not walk in his ways, he shall not bear his father's iniquity, for visiting of iniquity is tied to His haters, as I have mentioned. And the meaning of visiting is like remembering, as 'and HaShem visited Sarah' (Genesis 21:1), which is like 'and HaShem remembered,' for HaShem lengthens His anger toward the wicked, perhaps he will repent of the sin he sinned and beget a son who is better than he. And behold, if he walked in his father's ways — also the third generation, also the fourth generation — HaShem will not lengthen His anger to the fourth, if they were all thus for four generations, haters of HaShem, for in it the memory of all of them will be destroyed. For HaShem will remember what the father did, and what the son did, and the son of the son; therefore He will not lengthen to the fourth. And the sons of the sons are called sons; therefore it took a short way, for from the words 'third and fourth' a person will understand this. n: Reading לא יאריך אפו ('will not lengthen His anger') in both occurrences; some witnesses may read לא יאריך ('will not lengthen'), but the idiom requires אפו. Also, the phrase כי בו יאבד ('for in it…will be destroyed') is grammatically difficult and translated hyper-literally."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:36:14.426323844Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}